  {
   "id": "concept2",
   "type": "concept",
   "title": {
    "en": "Regulation and IC regulators"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "Load regulation measures how much the output falls from no load to full load: $\\%\\,\\text{Reg} = (V_{NL} - V_{FL})/V_{FL} \\times 100$; an ideal supply has 0 %. Line regulation measures the change in output for a change in input voltage. Three-terminal IC regulators make regulation easy: the 78xx series gives fixed positive outputs (7805 → +5 V, 7812 → +12 V), the 79xx series fixed negative outputs, and the LM317 an adjustable output. The input must be at least about 2 V above the output (the dropout voltage)."
   },
   "points": [
    {
     "en": "% Reg = (V_NL − V_FL)/V_FL × 100"
    },
    {
     "en": "78xx: +xx V; 79xx: −xx V"
    },
    {
     "en": "Input ≥ output + about 2 V"
    }
   ],
   "narration": {
